    Galaxy Miner is my first door written for OzMetro BBS and with any
    luck it won't be my last!  I hope you like it.  Details on how you
    play the game are provided internally for you, the sysop, and user
    alike.  What I will write here are some brief notes on what GMINER
    expects of you to get the game running on any OzMetro BBS.

    GMINER doesn't run standalone as yet, but I'll certainly be trying
    to remedy this for some later release!  However, the game WILL run
    on Transamiga BBS systems and probably a number of others too.  So
    give it a go, as it just may work on your setup too!


    GETTING STARTED

    This game is set up on OzMetro the same as for any door, and needs
    NO other files to run.  All you need do is place the 'door.exe' in
    a 'door' directory, and then configure a keypress on the menus for
    it.  Yep, as simple as that!  See the OzMetro documentation on how
    to set up doors if you need further information.  If you've set up
    a door or 2 on OzMetro already then you shouldn't have any problem
    at all setting this one up! 8^)


    OPTIONAL CONFIG

    A 'GMINER.cfg' file can also be installed in the same directory as
    the 'door.exe' which then allows you to adjust some of the default
    settings used by GMINER.  An example 'GMINER.cfg' file is enclosed
    for you within the archive.  The comments appended to each line of
    the config file can be left there for reference too; GMINER strips
    away the comments appended to any line of the config as long as it
    is AFTER a semi-colon!  If any lines are commented out completely,
    or left blank, or else a value entered is outside the given range,
    defaults will be used by GMINER in all cases.

    Additionally, any lines added at the bottom of the config file are
    completely ignored so you can add anything you like underneath the
    config itself!  Make sure, however, if you DO install 'GMINER.cfg'
    that EACH setting is found on the precise line the program expects
    it to be on.  For example, (LINE 12) is the ONLY line that will be
    checked for the number of entries GMINER will add to the highscore
    file before they start to scroll off.  


    CONFIG SETTINGS

    The following is a line by line explanation of the config file as
    used by GMINER. (Versions: 0.77-0.83)

    The setting on LINE 1: this sets the maximum time that a user with
    level 1 security can spend in the door.  (Not used by GMINER yet.)
    The default is 30 minutes and the minimum value is 10 minutes.  If
    however the time left on the BBS is less than this value, the time
    remaining on the BBS will override this setting.

    The setting on LINE 2: this sets the maximum time that a user with
    level 2 security can spend in the door.  (Not used by GMINER yet.)
    The default is 30 minutes and the minimum value is 10 minutes.  If
    however the time left on the BBS is less than this value, the time
    remaining on the BBS will override this setting.

    The setting on LINE 3: this sets the maximum time that a user with
    level 3 security can spend in the door.  (Not used by GMINER yet.)
    The default is 30 minutes and the minimum value is 10 minutes.  If
    however the time left on the BBS is less than this value, the time
    remaining on the BBS will override this setting.

    The setting on LINE 4: this sets the maximum time that a user with
    level 4 security can spend in the door.  (Not used by GMINER yet.)
    The default is 45 minutes and the minimum value is 10 minutes.  If
    however the time left on the BBS is less than this value, the time
    remaining on the BBS will override this setting.

    The setting on LINE 5: this sets the maximum time that a user with
    level 5 security can spend in the door.  (Not used by GMINER yet.)
    The default is 45 minutes and the minimum value is 10 minutes.  If
    however the time left on the BBS is less than this value, the time
    remaining on the BBS will override this setting.

    The setting on LINE 6: this sets the maximum time that a user with
    level 6 security can spend in the door.  (Not used by GMINER yet.)
    The default is 45 minutes and the minimum value is 10 minutes.  If
    however the time left on the BBS is less than this value, the time
    remaining on the BBS will override this setting.

    The setting on LINE 7: this sets the maximum time that a user with
    level 7 security can spend in the door.  (Not used by GMINER yet.)
    The default is 60 minutes and the minimum value is 10 minutes.  If
    however the time left on the BBS is less than this value, the time
    remaining on the BBS will override this setting.

    The setting on LINE 8: this sets the maximum time that a user with
    level 8 security can spend in the door.  (Not used by GMINER yet.)
    The default is 60 minutes and the minimum value is 10 minutes.  If
    however the time left on the BBS is less than this value, the time
    remaining on the BBS will override this setting.

    The setting on LINE 9: this sets the maximum time that a user with
    level 9 security can spend in the door.  (Not used by GMINER yet.)
    The default is 60 minutes and the minimum value is 10 minutes.  If
    however the time left on the BBS is less than this value, the time
    remaining on the BBS will override this setting.

    The setting on LINE 10: this sets the minimum user level which can
    access the alias functions of GMINER.  Its range is 1-9 as per the
    OzMetro user levels, and defaults to 2.  Any user with access at a
    level less than the value you put on this line will NOT be able to
    access the alias functions at all.

    The setting on LINE 11: this sets the minimum user level which can
    access the 'lastuser' file of GMINER.  Its range is 1-9 as per the
    OzMetro user levels, and defaults to 1.  Any user with access at a
    level less than the value you set on this line will NOT be able to
    see the 'lastuser' file.

    The setting on LINE 12: this is the maximum number of entries that
    GMINER is able to add to the highscore file before they will start
    to scroll off.  This value does NOT include the 3 line header!  So
    if you use 15 as the value on this line, the highscore file length
    will be actually 19 lines; 3 lines for the header, 15 score lines,
    and an EOF marker on the last blank line.  Values within the range
    10-200 lines will be accepted.  The default is for 34 score lines.
    (Which would be approximately 2 NTSC screens or 1.5 PAL screens.)

    The setting on LINE 13: this is the maximum number of entries that
    GMINER is able to add to the lastusers file before they will start
    to scroll off.  This value does NOT include the 3 line header!  So
    if you use 55 as the value on this line, the lastusers file length
    will be actually 59 lines; 3 lines for the header, 55 users lines,
    and an EOF marker on the last blank line.  Values within the range
    10-500 lines will be accepted.  The default is for 100 user lines.
    (Which would be approximately 5 NTSC screens or 4 PAL screens.)

    The setting on LINE 14: There is NO line 14, anything on, or below
    this line, is completely ignored by GMINER.


    EXAMPLE CONFIG

    10 ;LINE.1-maximum minutes for level 1 user (not used) (def.30)
    15 ;LINE.2-maximum minutes for level 2 user (not used) (def.30)
    40 ;LINE.3-maximum minutes for level 3 user (not used) (def.30)
    60 ;LINE.4-maximum minutes for level 4 user (not used) (def.45)
    30 ;LINE.5-maximum minutes for level 5 user (not used) (def.45)
    60 ;LINE.6-maximum minutes for level 6 user (not used) (def.45)
    60 ;LINE.7-maximum minutes for level 7 user (not used) (def.60)
    60 ;LINE.8-maximum minutes for level 8 user (not used) (def.60)
    90 ;LINE.9-maximum minutes for level 9 user (not used) (def.60)
    2  ;LINE10-minimum level for alias access.  (rg 1-9) (def.2)
    1  ;LINE11-minimum level for lastuser file. (rg 1-9) (def.1)
    20 ;LINE12-maximum entries: highscore file. (rg 10-200) (def.34)
    50 ;LINE13-maximum entries: lastusers file. (rg 10-500) (def.100)
    ;=====anything on this line or below is ignored


    FILES CREATED

    GMINER will create some files in its directory.  Examples of these
    are contained in the archive.  They are a 'GMINER.last' which is a
    list of ALL users who entered the door, and the other 'GMINER.high'
    is the high score file.  It is important you DO NOT change the top
    3 lines of either of these files otherwise GMINER will not be able
    to update them successfully.  You may delete any user lines, found
    below the 3 line header, or delete either or both files altogether,
    but don't change the 3 line header on the top in any way! 

    When GMINER updates either of the above 2 files, it will leave you
    a copy of the old file in the directory your door is running from.
    These will be named 'GMINER.last.old' and 'GMINER.high.old'.  Both
    files are only for safety, so delete them if you wish, or else you
    can just leave them there.  The maximum entries you want GMINER to
    maintain in these files can be set using the config!

    
    DIRECTORIES CREATED

    GMINER also creates 2 directories for itself in the door directory
    you have installed it in.  The first is 'chars': this directory is
    where any user aliases are stored.  Within the game you can choose
    an alias to use while playing, and GMINER will save this alias for
    you if you ask it to.  (GMINER has inbuilt checks for several RUDE
    words just in case any user decides to run amuck with them too!)

    The other directory created is for saved games.  While playing the
    game, GMINER asks the users if they wish to save their game at the
    completion of every year played.  If they do, a file is created in
    a directory called 'miners' which stores each player's details!  A
    user can quit half way through any game with this option, allowing
    them to continue at a later date.  It's also handy if there's some
    disaster that wipes them out in the next year, their time runs out
    or the power fails etc, as they can easily restore their game from
    the previous year if they did save their game during play! 8^)

    Saved games are deleted whenever a user completes a game as well.

    To see what happens go in and save both an alias, and also a game,
    and look at the files and directories created after doing this.  I
    have included some samples of these in the archive as well.  Allow
    about 2k of HD space for every user that has stored both the alias
    and a partial game file.  Every so often; you had better go in and
    check the creation date of these files and delete them accordingly
    until I can write something that will do this maintenance for you!


    CONCLUSION

    So that's about it!  Go in and play a game after you set it up and
    send me some feedback on what you think.  Send me a few $$$ if you
    can as this will be a great help as well!  I'll be supporting this
    game, and looking at others I can write too, so any suggestions on
    how I can improve this one, or ideas on other games you would like
    to see available, don't hesitate to tell me particulars etc. 8^)


